      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "The reference class/base rate is short-horizon persistence in this slowly moving beneficiary stock. The four first-release levels average 547.35 thousand, but the latest observation is 544.44 thousand and receives greater weight because the target is only two months ahead."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "The update is deliberately small and auditable: start from April persistence at 544.44 thousand; apply -1.50 thousand for the May unemployment-rate decline, only about 0.16 of one-month historical sigma because EI receipt is lagged; add +0.39 thousand for incomplete pass-through over the two-month horizon; assign 0.00 to temporary EI measures and tariff effects because the cited evidence does not quantify them. The signed adjustments sum to -1.11 thousand, giving 543.33 thousand."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Prior/update/interval: The model is latest-level persistence using January-April 2026 first prints. Successive changes were -12.72, +5.89 and -3.56 thousand; their sample standard deviation is sigma = 9.31 thousand. For a two-month horizon, independent-change scaling gives 9.31*sqrt(2) = 13.17 thousand and an 80% half-width of roughly 1.28*sigma_two_month = 16.86 thousand. Applied around 543.33, this independently implies bounds near 526.47 and 560.19 thousand. The ladder-implied bounds of 526.67 and 560.00 have half-widths of 16.66 and 16.67 thousand, effectively matching that volatility benchmark rather than determining it."
      },
      {
        "kind": "math",
        "text": "Ladder: P(X <= 520) = 0.03; P(X <= 525) = 0.08; P(X <= 530) = 0.14; P(X <= 535) = 0.25; P(X <= 540) = 0.40; P(X <= 545) = 0.55; P(X <= 550) = 0.68; P(X <= 555) = 0.80; P(X <= 560) = 0.90; P(X <= 565) = 0.95; P(X <= 570) = 0.98; P(X <= 575) = 0.99. Linear interpolation gives q10 = 525 + (0.10-0.08)/(0.14-0.08)*5 = 526.67, median = 540 + (0.50-0.40)/(0.55-0.40)*5 = 543.33, and q90 = 560.00 thousand."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Upside risk comes from delayed tariff-related layoffs, broader use of temporary EI measures, or slower exits from benefits; a first print above 560.00 thousand would land above the interval. Downside risk comes from sustained employment gains, faster return-to-work flows, or benefit exhaustion; a print below 526.67 thousand would land below the interval."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Review disposition: Accepted the volatility correction, independent two-month interval derivation, and signed adjustment breakdown. Reduced the unsupported upward update and retained the documented August 19 calendar versus August 20 ledger discrepancy while keeping the registered resolution date."
